From: fnerd@smds.com (FutureNerd Steve Witham)
Date: Mon, 18 Apr 94 12:37:53 EDT
Subject: [#94-4-127] Land vs Other Property

Dan Davis responds to some of Bo and Black Unicorn's...

> Very good points.
> 
> To be clear, however, the discussion over property rights in land is
> not whether to respect property rights as such (which I feel is a
> nonsensical question, if property is used with a sensible definition),
> but when to respect a claim to a property right. I need not treat
> something as someone else's property simply because they claim it is
> their property. Under what conditions does it makes sense for me to do
> so? Under what conditions should I lend my support/moral sanction to
> their property claim?

There's cold, grudging respect and warm, fuzzy respect.  I think you 
should respect people's property if it's going to be too hard to take 
and keep it.  Whether you give them moral (warm) support has to do with 
whether you've psychologically worked out a glowing picture of 
persnickety, greedy beings being generally nifty even so, and having 
mutual respect and cooperating.  (Also whether the particular situation 
fits into this glowing picture.)  I don't think someone can have this 
glowing picture as long as they hold onto resentment of the basic facts
of arbitrariness and raw power.  For me, knowing what I *think* about
property is easier with this picture and knowing what I *feel* about 
it.  It sorts out the important things from the legalistic quibbling 
that (it seems to me) comes from trying to use words to patch over a 
psychological unfinished spot.

Which is why I got tired of _Reason_-style libertarianism so quick and
became an anarchist...(another story).

-fnerd
