From: fnerd@smds.com (FutureNerd Steve Witham)
Date: Mon, 18 Apr 94 13:38:08 EDT
Subject: [#94-4-128] Land vs Other Property

i said-

> > ...I think the "effective defense" point of view is the best
> > way to see which kinds of arrangements will work out long-term.

& Dan Davis replied-

> If "effective defense" includes the social processes, then I agree.

Yes, including the social situation, but finally only to the extent 
that it affects the "effective defense".

> The mutual agreement I was talking about is not an actual face to face
> discussion, but a case where each side sees it in his interest to
> support the property system. 

Okay, but I think this is going too abstract in one jump.  It's 
important to see the long continuum from a literal tug-of-war,
to a shootout, to an exchange of threats, to a temporary two-party
truce, to two neighbors getting along peacibly, to a larger group
where there are third-party ostracism and gossip sanctions, to a
settlement with some locally-improvised laws and a sheriff, to a country 
with abstract concepts and traditions of "property."

> The use of force becomes largely driven by social sanction.  When you 
> and I disagree over a claimed property right, one or the other of us 
> in some part sanctions force. 

Having trouble understanding, because (as in my list above) I see
social sanction as "driven by" (deriving from) force, and because I 
sanction that force, period, although there are decisions about when 
and how to use it.  If you're trying to draw a distinction between
using force and not, I'm trying to say it's only whether the soft gloves
are on or off (and I mean that in the nicest way!)

> [other good stuff]

> > So even though the whole thing's about force, it's not 
> > *brute* force that wins.
> 
> Well, its not the brute force that I can personally deliver with my
> body that counts. Nor is it the force which I can personally control.
> It is the force which myself and others bring to bear that counts.

The total, not just any subset.  But also, by "not brute" I mean not
unsophisticated, unsubtle, uncivilized, insensitive, etc.  ("Not un"--
Orwell forgive me.)

> [good stuff]
> 
> Yet the landless are not powerless in this regard. While it is true
> that any particular fellow has little hope of overcoming the system
> through force, the weight of their moral sanction, if withdrawn, would
> make a big difference. People vote, they argue, they persuade, they
> snicker, they write. These are the main powers used to keep the
                       ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
> current system in place, and they would be the main powers
  ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
> overthrowing it. 

Once again, you're focusing on "the system," something monolythic and
abstractly justified or disputed.  At best this kind of system is icing 
on the layer cake.  It can be good practically, and it can recognize 
universal truth.  But it's bad if either the people for or against it 
forget that it isn't the basic reality itself.  Bad for the "landless" 
rebels in your case if they succeed in changing "the system" only to 
find that it was based on a more concrete, stubborn reality underneath.
Bad for people who twist it to support the status quo, when the
economy suffers as a result, or when this or that revolution comes.

