Subject: [#94-4-129] land property

Dan (thread 3) says-

> When I said symmetrical, I was referring to a common starting ground
> of no physical force sanctioned against others. You can't hit me, I
> can't hit you. A claim to property is a claim to the right to use
> physical force in certain situations.  Nothing wrong with such claims,
> but I think it is the claimant of such a prerogative who bears the
> burden of proof.

Hokay, my serious alternative: you *can* hit me, I *can* hit you.  We 
usually *don't*, but that just shows that the system works.  I find my 
system simpler than yours--nothing is prohibited--and just as 
symmetrical.

i had said-

> > I proposed a more symmetrical (to me) system
> > where everybody is taxed for everything they own, but I don't think
> > you've said much about that.
> 
> Sorry, I missed that part, and assumed you were simply opposed to my
> land tax argument. 

No, opposed but interested, trying to draw you out.

> Well, I may get to argue in favor of certain
> property rights now. I don't agree to equally taxing all property. On
> this point, I'll have to bear the burden of proof.

To be clear, I was just saying, as long as you have this argument 
against land property, why not be symmetrical and apply it to all 
property?  I think if you can make some arguments for some kinds of
property, it will illuminate your position on land.

> > The main large-scale
> > change I'd make is to take away institutions that tax everyone and
> > enforce the same (albeit slanted) rules on everyone.  

[my real wish]

> Umm, didn't you just come out for a tax on all property? Was that a
> little joke? 

"Reductio ad absurdum."  Not to make fun of you but to provide 
something to talk about.

> Ah yes, well our little show may rapidly be drawing to an end. 

I hope not!

> You see, I'm not an anarchist, and don't find markets in defense
> meaningful outside some context of rights.

Ahh!  Light dawns!  You see, I'm an anarchist, and I don't find 
"rights" meaningful outside of a social situation involving trade,
negotiation and force--in other words a market in defense.  I am not
joking, this is my exact point.  "Rights" to me are an abstraction
that has gone so far for so long that it's become uprooted and
reified in people's minds, which is why it's so hard to discuss.

> I hope it doesn't come to
> "let the market decide what kind of property rights are involved". You
> are part of the market. What do you decide?

Property happens naturally with its own dynamic, but it can happen
more or less happilly, and people talking about it effect that.  The 
strange thing is I think I feel more optimistic about changing the 
world than you do.  

Getting to a point where the market would decide, would be *much* 
different from what we have now.  Actually, a perfect market is an
ideal that can never be reached because it involves "zero transaction
cost" and infinite clairvoiance.  But it's a grand ideal, with
lots of room for creativity (which I ain't got enough of, so that's 
part of why I haven't been too concrete--but mostly we need to find
some common ground and topics interesting to us both.)

